[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged on subject lines ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject))
and marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Read by tenant, channel and booking form, the way
`causes.ts` already reads them, its reports are three different problems. Each needs its own ticket,
and one fix will not close the others.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. A fix for the parser closes the parser problem only. The other two stay open until
they have tickets and fixes of their own.
</Callout>

## `parser`: dates read month first

The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes the day first, so a
booking for 7 April comes out as 4 July. These reports come in through the app, by email and through
booking-v2 forms, at every tenant.

## `hint`: booking-v3 tells people to type month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. At a day-first tenant, people type what the hint
tells them, month first, and the form then reads the date the tenant's way. These are only reports
that came through the form at a booking-v3 tenant. Fixing the parser does not fix them: the hint and
the way the form reads its own field have to agree.

## `one-day move`: off by one day, not flipped

The desk set these reports apart in its own notes (`desk-notes.json`): the date moves by one day, or
lands on a different day for different people, instead of swapping day and month. Examples:
R-41202 (Tuesday 12 May shown as Monday 11 May), R-41344 (one meeting, a different day for a client in
Lisbon and one in Toronto), R-41236 (the invite arrived before the booking was made). The cause is not
known yet. Until it is, these reports are a problem of their own, not a footnote under `parser`.

## Intake tags

`meta.intakeTag` is what intake guessed from the reporter's browser. The note under the table lists
the tags intake has, counted from the data; none of them is `one-day move`. The column "intake tagged as" in the table
shows what intake tagged each problem's reports as. In the `parser` and `hint` rows, the other tag is
a wrong guess. In the `one-day move` row, the tag is not a mismatch: intake had no tag to give. Triage
by tenant, channel and form, not by the tag.

## What changes

- Each problem gets its own ticket, linked in the table above.
- #4471 should not be closed by the parser fix alone.
- Merging on subject alone put these problems into one ticket. Decision 0001 stays as it is; a new
  decision record should replace it.

